{
  "correction_rules": [
    "Persistent positive bias: model forecasts are systematically above realized prices across most horizons; apply a downward level shift (stronger in the first ~1–20 days).",
    "Downtrend underreaction / lag: when recent history is falling, the model does not drop fast enough (keeps prices too high early in the path). Increase negative slope in the first 1–15 days after a clear bearish trend in the last 2–4 weeks.",
    "Over-optimistic rebounds: the model frequently predicts mean-reversion upward that does not materialize (or is delayed). Dampen rebound amplitude, especially after multi-day selloffs; require confirmation (stabilization) before projecting recovery.",
    "Horizon shape error: early-horizon errors are larger and mostly upward; later horizons still biased but less so. Use horizon-dependent correction (bigger downward adjustment for days 1–10, moderate for days 11–20, small for days 21–30).",
    "Volatility overshoot: forecast paths are too ‘bouncy’ versus outcomes (higher peaks during declines). Apply volatility shrinkage toward a smoother, monotone continuation of the prevailing trend unless recent realized volatility is rising."
  ]
}
